    "case_summary": "A 58-year-old African American male attended a hepatology clinic for a routine liver ultrasound due to a history of hepatitis C. The patient reported mild, intermittent upper abdominal pain, which was consistent with his history of gastroesophageal reflux disease. He did not experience weight loss, changes in appetite or bowel habits, jaundice, or general malaise. His medical history included hyperlipidemia, peptic ulcer disease, and hepatitis C (antibody positive, PCR negative). Physical examination and laboratory studies were unremarkable. An abdominal ultrasound revealed a well-defined mass measuring 3.4 × 2.9 cm in the head of the pancreas. A subsequent computed tomography (CT) scan confirmed an enhancing mass in the same location. No signs of disease outside the pancreas were detected. Tumor-associated antigen levels (CEA, CA 19-9, and CA-125) were within normal ranges. An endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) guided needle biopsy was performed, but the pathological findings were inconclusive. Due to ongoing concerns, a diagnostic laparoscopy was conducted, followed by a surgical procedure. The patient recovered well and was discharged on the 15th postoperative day. He is currently under close outpatient surveillance without additional treatment.\n\nMicroscopic examination revealed that all were hyperplastic lymph nodes. The remaining surgical margins were negative. This lymphoid lesion within the pancreatic head showed marked vascular proliferation and hyalinization of germinal centers. Involuted germinal centers were surrounded by concentric rings of small lymphocytes. i.e. 'onion-skinning' penetrated by hyalinized vessels. Moreover, the interfollicular zones were rich in plasma cells and hyalinized vasculature. Distribution of T and B cell markers (CD20, CD3, CD45) was normal and markers indicative of lymphoid malignancies (i.e. CD30, CD15, CD5, CD10, BCL-2) did not show evidence of such entities. PCR analysis of paraffin-embedded tissue revealed no clonal rearrangement of the immunoglobulin heavy chain or T-cell receptor gamma and beta chain genes. MALT1 (18q21) clonal rearrangement by FISH was negative.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
